# CHANGELOG

## 20260806

0.4.1: Document the Changes section which tier 1 reads.

1. ~ README.md: + The Changes section, being cumulative across one minor series and restarted at each new one so that it cannot grow without bound, what "Changes since 0.5:" names, what the -/0 and n/n+1 markers mean and why the dash, that items are numbered continuously across the whole section, that only the last marker's items become the entry, and what ends the section. It was the least recoverable part of the format, being visible in the headers but nowhere stated.
2. ~ README.md: Name the tiers where they are described. The script header calls them tiers, and the README twice refers to them by number, but the description itself never introduced the term.


## 20260806

0.4.0: Write the CHANGELOG in the form the repositories already use.

1. ~ format_entry(): A "## <date>" heading, with the version and summary on the first line of the body and the items unindented, in place of a "<version> - <date>" heading with indented items. git-import-all reads this directly, so the output no longer wants converting by hand before an import.
2. ~ write_changelog(): Two blank lines between entries rather than one, so that the boundaries stay visible where items run to several sentences.
3. + heading_date(): Warn on stderr naming the revision, and write ???????? where the script header carries no date, rather than inventing a date or stopping the run. The output is a seed to be edited, so an obvious hole in it is honest.
4. + summary_line()
5. - version_label(): The -/0 and n/n+1 markers exist because a Changes section spans a whole minor series, restarted at each one to keep it from growing without bound. Once every version has its own dated entry there is nothing left for them to disambiguate. They are still parsed out of the headers, merely no longer written.


## 20260806

0.3.0: Add a README and a TODO, and retire the Changes section from the script header.

1. + README.md: What the tool is for, the three tiers and the order they are tried in, the skip on an existing CHANGELOG, the output layout, the relationship to git-import-all and git-boot, and a note on this repository's own CHANGELOG having been seeded by the tool.
2. + TODO.txt: The four items which had been kept as a loose "changelogger roadmap" file alongside the revisions, where git-import-all would have taken it for a revision of its own had it not begun ignoring everything but numbered directories.
3. - The Changes section from the script header. The CHANGELOG holds that record now, and keeping both invites them to disagree. Revisions 0 to 2 keep theirs, being the source the CHANGELOG was seeded from.


